@startuml keeper-sign
!theme plain
title Key keeper (v1.3): a process asks for a signature over a local socket; the keeper identifies the caller by peer UID and signs with that identity's key; the private key never leaves the keeper

participant "Agent process\n(OS user a_7)" as A
participant "Unix socket\n/var/run/swarma/keeper.sock\n(mode 0666)" as S
participant "Key keeper\n(privileged daemon)" as KK
database "Master secret\n(Secure Enclave / TPM / DPAPI)" as HS
database "Registry\n(identity → public key, UID map)" as R

A -> S: connect; request sign{bytes_sha256, purpose}
S -> KK: connection + peer credentials (uid 5017, pid)
KK -> KK: uid 5017 → identity path product/P/a_7'\n(map written by the dispatcher at register)
alt uid unknown or identity revoked
  KK --> A: refused{unknown_uid | revoked}
else known
  KK -> HS: derive(path) inside the secure boundary (hardened child)
  HS --> KK: signing capability for this operation
  KK -> KK: sign canonical bytes; audit line {uid, identity, purpose, digest, at}
  KK --> A: signature {alg ed25519, key_id, value}
end

== other callers ==
note over KK
  register(uid, path): dispatcher only (its own UID, grant user.create)
  revoke(identity): owner's signed act or the dispatcher at retire
  pubkey(identity): anyone
  decrypt(blob): the identity's own UID only
  The bridge signs on behalf of a posting agent only with that
  agent's bound identity; the keeper checks the binding record.
end note

== what cannot happen ==
note over A, HS
  No API returns a private key. A prompt-injected agent can ask for
  signatures of what it is allowed to send, and nothing else: the
  signature request is audited, rate-limited and tied to the identity
  the kernel reports, not to anything the agent claims.
end note
@enduml
